#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285193 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dcbd56f-0fc2-59c5-a016-4272be4734c7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points at Which a Function is Not Differentiable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e430fd9-fc23-5456-83e1-9c093a8e5bc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285195} {:answer/id #uuid "7ad87844-6c35-5685-9feb-63269ad45b2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285196} {:answer/id #uuid "63e99eb8-84f1-5ef8-be7b-e964a4e34726" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285197} {:answer/id #uuid "9608d249-ff60-5ec9-afa3-323309b776f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285198} {:answer/id #uuid "43c57746-d217-5cd3-b82a-f65897436695" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285199}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9608d249-ff60-5ec9-afa3-323309b776f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285198} :answer-field/id #uuid "802981d9-5f74-590b-b09c-15247a914e2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c0c690a4-aac6-53e2-93ef-bbe0c027b941" :question/math-academy-id "q-101997" :question/problem "Given that a function $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=-5$, is differentiable at $x=-2$, and has a  vertical tangent at $x=0$, which of the following statements must be true for **any** $f(x)$ that satisfy these criteria?\n\n- It is **not** differentiable at $x=-5$\n- It  is continuous at $x=-2$\n- It is differentiable at $x=0$" :question/worked-solution "Differentiability implies continuity. Hence,\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-5$, it can't be differentiable there either.\n\n- Statement II is true.   Since $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x=-2$ it is also  continuous at $x=-2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since we have a vertical tangent at $x=0$, the function $f(x)$ is not differentiable at this point."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115793 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b4100f8-8a66-5243-b0b6-c36b9d17dac3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Which Functions are Differentiable at a Given Point From the Function Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df482595-47a1-5ef1-af22-1d9bdb814d80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114130} {:answer/id #uuid "099e5f84-136e-523b-aa0b-8f33ceb4dbbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114131} {:answer/id #uuid "0dacff7d-ebdf-523d-9c12-623c069f0a66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114132} {:answer/id #uuid "210062db-cbaa-5166-aabc-19a294ea34ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114133} {:answer/id #uuid "7ad50e2d-95f3-5123-9d6f-16799295bc00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114134}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "099e5f84-136e-523b-aa0b-8f33ceb4dbbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114131} :answer-field/id #uuid "304a18c6-3619-5656-b80d-6491b5bc8a66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114129}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "995f3b18-ad68-56fb-a94d-9e7f0a50f6c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-47367" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function given below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x\\ne 1 \\\\ 2, & x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- It is continuous and differentiable at $x=1$\n- It is **not** continuous at $x=1$ but it is differentiable at $x=1$\n- It is **not** differentiable on the interval $x\\in (-2,2)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement individually.\n\n- Statement I is false. First, we investigate the continuity of $f(x)$ . It is continuous everywhere except, possibly, $x=1.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}{x}^{2}={1}^{2}=1 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}{x}^{2}={1}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne f(1)=2,\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1$ (it is a removable discontinuity).\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1,$ it is also not differentiable there.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1,$ it is not differentiable on any interval that includes this point. Consequently, it is not differentiable in $x\\in (-2,2).$\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285200 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "794398c0-ffbf-5971-a411-f236607f56b8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function is Differentiable at a Point Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0132a0a-2d48-5474-8ff0-2006be8cd30b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285202} {:answer/id #uuid "236bd77a-fe48-50f8-8aba-49c8e6cbfa7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285203} {:answer/id #uuid "a565db49-640b-5346-bd78-c62d22fa9fd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285204} {:answer/id #uuid "0dc4e6e1-b458-57e6-a617-8bb50b8686cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285205} {:answer/id #uuid "f647ee41-39b8-56e5-9de1-a37ee0ea1d03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f647ee41-39b8-56e5-9de1-a37ee0ea1d03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285206} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e52457a-2386-5a3b-a371-cc79ad18a5eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285201}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9426dbc5-fee7-5af9-83b3-97f66b9b1d25" :question/math-academy-id "q-73249" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996853/assets/ec8101640f2bd84506d1c47b92f088675080d87eafff4000a4f3e0d14ccebb55.png)\n\nGiven the plot of the function $y=f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The function is continuous at $x=-2$\n- The function is continuous at $x=1$\n- The function is differentiable at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "- Statement I is false. From the graph we can see that $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=-2.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The graph can be drawn in a neighborhood of $x=1$ without taking the pencil off the paper.\n\n- Statement III is false. The graph of $f(x)$ shows a cusp at this point and so it is not differentiable there.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285207 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dcbd56f-0fc2-59c5-a016-4272be4734c7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points at Which a Function is Not Differentiable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35e89a85-3627-59f7-b95e-3e90d18f30ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285209} {:answer/id #uuid "fd1aa557-95f0-5485-b2b2-3bfe1c84f2e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285210} {:answer/id #uuid "1f4f6ed6-8a29-5f9b-b2ef-ae62fe125854" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285211} {:answer/id #uuid "bb2f1210-8ec9-5abc-967f-f8c08c42095c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285212} {:answer/id #uuid "0dcc79b2-5ee4-563c-a5e1-e07f28af2bab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb2f1210-8ec9-5abc-967f-f8c08c42095c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285212} :answer-field/id #uuid "eaf00d60-8c08-5d22-9496-a7172d67adad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "de43e357-7e2a-5e20-ae7c-f5be72dd85e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-73260" :question/problem "Given that a function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$, has a corner point at $x=2,$ and is differentiable at $x=4$, which of the following statements must be true for **any** $f(x)$ that satisfy these criteria?\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)=f(4).$\n- It is **not** differentiable at $x=2$\n- It is differentiable at $x=0$" :question/worked-solution "- Statement I is true. Differentiability implies continuity. Hence, ${lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)=f(4).$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a corner point at $x=2$, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- Statement III is false.  Continuity does not imply differentiability. For instance, the function  $g(x)=|x|$ is continuous at $x=0$ but it is not differentiable there."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285214 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b4100f8-8a66-5243-b0b6-c36b9d17dac3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Which Functions are Differentiable at a Given Point From the Function Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4dd4f175-0e2d-52a0-bd1d-cec2f642f503" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285216} {:answer/id #uuid "c7bbc16d-af0b-5881-b70b-ad8052c62453" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=\\frac{1}{x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285217} {:answer/id #uuid "3cf073db-21c6-5479-8835-b1bc8ee8422d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p(x)=\\sqrt{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285218} {:answer/id #uuid "7ddb2ced-7fc7-5bff-b3de-7d7b9ce84f77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h(x)=2|x-1|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285219} {:answer/id #uuid "91b223e8-060c-5caf-bf71-5a173edd4138" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x,\\, & x<1 \\\\ x+2,\\, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cf073db-21c6-5479-8835-b1bc8ee8422d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p(x)=\\sqrt{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285218} :answer-field/id #uuid "8809e040-3a4f-5b40-93ee-ddba22f73a0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285215}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3e1e5ddc-8e90-5bdf-a10e-7ce2c1147053" :question/math-academy-id "q-73264" :question/problem "Which of the following functions is differentiable at the point $x=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the function $p(x)$ is differentiable for all $x>-1.$ Therefore, the correct answer is $p(x)=\\sqrt{x+1}.$\n\nThe other functions are not differentiable at $x=1.$\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=1$ and hence it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $h(x)$ has a corner point at $x=1$ and hence it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $q(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=1$ and hence it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $g(x)$ has a vertical tangent at $x=1$, and hence it is not differentiable there."}]}
